<BlogPost
  title="Minimalism"
  description="The philosophy of minimalism that shaped my approach to design, code, and life - from UNIX principles to GNOME aesthetics."
  date="2025-10-06"
  image="/images/minimal.jpg"
  slug="minimalism"
>
<p>
    I've always liked simple things. It took me a while to realise
    how much that preference shaped the way I design, write code,
    and approach everyday life.
</p>

<p>
    I'm 22 now, a final-year Computer Science student. I liked the
    idea of "less is more" long before I knew anything about design
    principles. When I first came across UNIX-like systems, their
    simplicity clicked with me. Each command had a clear purpose,
    and nothing felt unnecessary.
</p>

<p>
    I learned design on my laptop through YouTube tutorials,
    without taking any formal courses. I started with posters and
    got better by trying things out. Some of that work eventually
    turned into small paid gigs. Through it all, I kept coming
    back to clean layouts with enough space for each element to
    breathe.
</p>

<p>
    Back in 2017, when I was active on Indian Amino, I was always
    pushing for simpler, more balanced designs. Some people saw
    that as plain or lazy, but I liked the challenge of making a
    design work with fewer elements. For me, it meant keeping
    what mattered.
</p>

<p>
    That approach carried over into programming. I wanted my code
    to be clear and easy to follow, so I tried to keep unnecessary
    complexity out of it. Simple, solid code has always appealed
    to me.
</p>

<p>
    Around the same time, I discovered GNOME. Its clean interface
    and balanced design felt right to me, and I liked having fewer
    distractions. It became my favourite Linux desktop, and I
    chose it for my first major development setup. It was
    comfortable to use, and its design was close to what I wanted
    in my own work.
</p>

<p>
    The same approach guided my time as president of The FOSS Club.
    My tenure ended a few months ago, but I still come back to KISS
    (Keep It Simple, Stupid). Whether I'm writing code, designing
    something, or organising a project, I try to keep it simple
    and useful.
</p>

<p>
    Simple and beautiful. That's how I like it, and how I'd like
    it to stay.
</p>
</BlogPost>
